Sure! To update a `break` node’s label to `"loopEnd"` only when it isn’t already set to that value, you can use the `updateBreak` function like this:

```javascript
// Assume you have the break node in string form (e.g., JSON or a custom string representation)
const breakNodeString = /* your break node string here */;

// Call the updateBreak function
const updatedNode = updateBreak({
  node: breakNodeString,
  label: "loopEnd"
});

// `updatedNode` now contains the updated break node (or the original if no change was needed)
```

If you already have the node as a JavaScript object instead of a string, just stringify it before passing it to the function. Let me know if you’d like help converting your node to the required string format!
